Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "1923-austrian-legislative-election":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"1923 Austrian legislative election",category:"Elections",eyebrow:"Second National Council election · 21 October 1923",infoboxKicker:"Election during the Geneva stabilization programme",landscape:nationalCouncil1923,
    lead:"The 1923 Austrian legislative election returned Chancellor Ignaz Seipel’s Christian Social Party as the largest force in the National Council. The Christian Socials won 82 of the reduced chamber’s 165 seats, the Social Democratic Workers’ Party 68, German-national parties 10, and the Landbund five. The result preserved a conservative governing majority without giving any party sole control and placed the republic’s financial reconstruction before an electorate still divided into durable political camps.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7s3g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:30:00-06:00 -->The election, campaign, result, party distribution, and resulting second legislative period are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Its position between the 1920 founding election and the more polarized 1927 Unity List contest is historical chronology and interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"reconstruction",title:"Election under financial reconstruction",html:"<p><a href='ignaz-seipel.html'>Ignaz Seipel</a> entered the campaign one year after the <a href='geneva-protocols-1922.html'>Geneva Protocols</a> had secured an international loan, fiscal supervision, and a programme of state retrenchment. The government presented currency stabilization and restored foreign credit as proof that Christian Social leadership had prevented collapse.</p><p>Opponents attached the same programme to civil-service dismissals, falling real incomes, unemployment, and outside control. The election therefore judged both the necessity of reconstruction and the distribution of its costs.</p>"},
      {id:"chamber",title:"A smaller National Council",html:"<p>The electoral reform reduced the <a href='national-council-first-republic.html'>National Council</a> from 183 to 165 seats. Proportional representation continued, and 83 mandates were required for an absolute majority.</p><p>The second legislative period began on 20 November 1923. Its smaller chamber did not simplify the party system: Catholic-conservative, Social Democratic, German-national, and agrarian organizations still represented distinct social institutions outside Parliament.</p>"},
      {id:"campaign",title:"Campaign and political camps",html:"<p>The <a href='christian-social-party-austria.html'>Christian Social Party</a> defended stabilization, Catholic interests, the provinces, and the exclusion of Social Democracy from federal government. The <a href='social-democratic-workers-party-austria.html'>SDAP</a> attacked austerity while pointing to municipal construction and welfare in <a href='red-vienna.html'>Red Vienna</a> as an alternative use of public power.</p><p>Greater Germans combined national union, secular and commercial constituencies, and opposition to clerical rule. The Landbund appealed to agricultural voters who wanted an autonomous rural voice rather than absorption into the larger bourgeois parties.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Result",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Party or electoral group</th><th>Votes</th><th>Share</th><th>Seats</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Christian Social Party</td><td>1,459,047</td><td>44.05%</td><td>82</td></tr><tr><td>Social Democratic Workers’ Party</td><td>1,311,870</td><td>39.60%</td><td>68</td></tr><tr><td>Greater German–Landbund list</td><td>259,375</td><td>7.83%</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Landbund</td><td>99,583</td><td>3.01%</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Other lists</td><td>182,731</td><td>5.51%</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Turnout was 87.05 percent. Christian Socials finished one seat short of a majority, while a conservative coalition remained numerically straightforward.</p>"},
      {id:"government",title:"Seipel’s renewed mandate",html:"<p>Seipel continued as chancellor with Greater German support. His cabinet could legislate without the SDAP, but no coalition partner ceased to possess leverage: defections, cabinet disputes, and financial scandal could still remove a government between elections.</p><p>When Seipel resigned in November 1924, <a href='rudolf-ramek.html'>Rudolf Ramek</a> inherited the same parliamentary basis. The voters had elected a legislature, not guaranteed a single cabinet for its full term.</p>"},
      {id:"term",title:"The second legislative period",html:"<p>The elected chamber enacted the Schilling conversion, financial and administrative reforms, social insurance measures, and the budgets of stabilization. It also witnessed banking failures, rising unemployment, and intensifying paramilitary organization.</p><p>The SDAP increased its vote while remaining excluded from cabinet. That combination—electoral growth without access to federal government—encouraged both patient parliamentary strategy and anxiety that ordinary majority rule would never be allowed to operate.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"From stabilization to polarization",html:"<p>The 1923 result showed that fiscal crisis had not dissolved Austria’s established camps. The government survived reconstruction electorally, but its majority depended on alliances among parties with different positions on Germany, agriculture, religion, and economic policy.</p><p>By the <a href='1927-austrian-legislative-election.html'>next election</a>, Christian Socials and Greater Germans would suppress those differences inside a Unity List designed principally to prevent a Social Democratic plurality.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"1920-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Previous contest",label:"1920 Legislative Election"},{href:"1927-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Following contest",label:"1927 Legislative Election"},{href:"ignaz-seipel.html",kicker:"Chancellor and campaign leader",label:"Ignaz Seipel"},{href:"geneva-protocols-1922.html",kicker:"Economic settlement",label:"Geneva Protocols"},{href:"rudolf-ramek.html",kicker:"Later chancellor",label:"Rudolf Ramek"},{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Chamber elected",label:"National Council"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/statistiken/personen-statistiken/zusammensetzung-nr/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — 1923 National Council composition and legislative period"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1923_Austrian_legislative_election",label:"Election data compiled from Nohlen and Stöver, Elections in Europe"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1923_Austrian_Nationalrat.svg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — 1923 National Council diagram and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Election date","21 October 1923"],["Turnout","87.05%"],["Seats","165"],["Majority","83"],["Christian Social Party","82 seats · 44.05%"],["Social Democratic Workers’ Party","68 seats · 39.60%"],["German-national parties","10 seats"],["Landbund","5 seats"],["Government after election","Ignaz Seipel’s conservative coalition"],["Legislative period","20 November 1923–18 May 1927"]]
  }),

  "1927-austrian-legislative-election":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"1927 Austrian legislative election",category:"Elections",eyebrow:"Third National Council election · 24 April 1927",infoboxKicker:"Unity List contest against Social Democracy",landscape:nationalCouncil1927,
    lead:"The 1927 Austrian legislative election returned the Unity List of the Christian Social and Greater German parties with 85 of 165 National Council seats. The Social Democratic Workers’ Party increased its representation to 71 and the Landbund won nine. Held after the Schattendorf killings and amid visible paramilitary mobilization, the election gave Chancellor Ignaz Seipel a narrow conservative majority while confirming that the two mass political camps together commanded more than nine tenths of represented voters.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8t4h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:35:00-06:00 -->The election, campaign, result, Unity List coalition, and legislature it produced are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The third National Council’s 1929 constitutional amendment, 1930 dissolution, and the effect of the July 1927 violence upon its political memory are likewise inherited historical chronology and interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"polarization",title:"The polarized republic",html:"<p>The <a href='1923-austrian-legislative-election.html'>previous election</a> had preserved Christian Social-led government while Social Democracy grew in opposition. Municipal Vienna, Catholic provincial institutions, unions, newspapers, cooperatives, veterans’ organizations, and armed formations gave each camp a social reach extending far beyond parliamentary membership.</p><p>The <a href='schattendorf-incident.html'>Schattendorf incident</a> of 30 January 1927, in which right-wing Frontkämpfer killed a disabled veteran and a child, made political violence an immediate campaign question.</p>"},
      {id:"unity",title:"The Unity List",html:"<p>Seipel joined the Christian Social Party and the Greater German People’s Party in the Einheitsliste. The alliance aimed to pool conservative and German-national votes against the SDAP while permitting its components to retain separate parliamentary identities after election.</p><p>Official chamber composition therefore records 73 Christian Social and 12 German-national deputies, although the ballot alliance won 85 seats as a single list. Treating all 85 as Christian Social obscures the coalition’s construction.</p>"},
      {id:"opposition",title:"Social Democrats and agrarians",html:"<p>The SDAP campaigned on the record of <a href='red-vienna.html'>Red Vienna</a>, social protection, republican legality, and resistance to clerical and paramilitary reaction. Its <a href='linz-programme-1926.html'>Linz Programme</a> supplied a comprehensive reform platform but also language about dictatorial resistance that opponents used as evidence of revolutionary intent.</p><p>The Landbund remained outside the Unity List and offered rural voters an agrarian alternative. Minor antisemitic, National Socialist, Communist, minority, and anti-corruption lists won no seat.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Result",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>List</th><th>Votes</th><th>Share</th><th>Seats</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unity List (Christian Social–Greater German)</td><td>1,756,761</td><td>48.24%</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>Social Democratic Workers’ Party</td><td>1,539,635</td><td>42.28%</td><td>71</td></tr><tr><td>Landbund</td><td>230,157</td><td>6.32%</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Other lists</td><td>114,973</td><td>3.16%</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Turnout reached 89.27 percent. The Unity List held two seats more than an absolute majority, leaving every internal disagreement potentially consequential.</p>"},
      {id:"cabinet",title:"A narrow governing majority",html:"<p>Seipel remained chancellor. The result denied the SDAP federal office despite another advance in votes and seats, reinforcing the belief among conservative leaders that coalition geometry could contain the largest urban movement.</p><p>The Unity List was an electoral device rather than a merger. Christian Social, Greater German, and agrarian interests continued to bargain separately over ministries, tariffs, schools, constitutional reform, and relations with Germany.</p>"},
      {id:"july",title:"The July crisis",html:"<p>On 14 July a Vienna jury acquitted the defendants in the Schattendorf case. The following day, mass protest, the burning of the Palace of Justice, and police gunfire produced the <a href='july-revolt-1927.html'>July Revolt</a>, with scores killed and hundreds wounded.</p><p>The violence was not part of the election result, yet it fixed the meaning of the new legislature. Conservatives cited insurrection; Social Democrats cited a lethal police state and the failure of republican justice.</p>"},
      {id:"third",title:"The third legislative period",html:"<p>The National Council operated from 18 May 1927 to 1 October 1930. It survived cabinet changes from Seipel to <a href='ernst-streeruwitz.html'>Ernst Streeruwitz</a> and <a href='johannes-schober.html'>Johannes Schober</a>, and it passed the <a href='federal-constitutional-amendment-1929.html'>1929 constitutional amendment</a> through negotiated compromise.</p><p>President Wilhelm Miklas dissolved it in October 1930 at the government’s proposal—the only presidential dissolution of a National Council in the republic’s history.</p>"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"Electoral strength and constitutional weakness",html:"<p>The election demonstrated exceptionally high participation and stable mass allegiance. It did not resolve who possessed legitimate authority when electoral, municipal, police, and paramilitary power pointed in different directions.</p><p>Later authoritarian accounts used polarization to portray parliamentary failure as inevitable. The legislature’s subsequent compromises show that institutional politics continued, even as armed threats made every compromise more fragile.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"1923-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Previous contest",label:"1923 Legislative Election"},{href:"1930-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Following contest",label:"1930 Legislative Election"},{href:"schattendorf-incident.html",kicker:"Campaign context",label:"Schattendorf Incident"},{href:"july-revolt-1927.html",kicker:"Post-election crisis",label:"July Revolt"},{href:"ignaz-seipel.html",kicker:"Unity List leader",label:"Ignaz Seipel"},{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Chamber elected",label:"National Council"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/statistiken/personen-statistiken/zusammensetzung-nr/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — 1927 party composition and third legislative period"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/III/NRSITZ/2/imfname_798694.pdf",label:"Austrian Parliament — opening debate of the third National Council, 19 May 1927"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1927_Austrian_legislative_election",label:"Election data compiled from Nohlen and Stöver, Elections in Europe"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1927_Austrian_Nationalrat.svg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — 1927 National Council diagram and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Election date","24 April 1927"],["Turnout","89.27%"],["Seats","165"],["Majority","83"],["Unity List","85 seats · 48.24%"],["Unity List composition","73 Christian Social; 12 German-national deputies"],["Social Democratic Workers’ Party","71 seats · 42.28%"],["Landbund","9 seats · 6.32%"],["Chancellor after election","Ignaz Seipel"],["Legislative period","18 May 1927–1 October 1930"]]
  }),

  "michael-mayr":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"Michael Mayr",category:"People",eyebrow:"Historian, constitutional drafter, and chancellor · 1864–1922",infoboxKicker:"First federal chancellor under the 1920 constitution",landscape:mayrPortrait,
    lead:"Michael Mayr was an Austrian historian, archivist, Christian Social politician, and principal architect of the federal compromise embodied in the 1920 constitution. He led the government from July 1920 to June 1921 and became Austria’s first federal chancellor when the constitution entered force. His cabinet completed the transition from the provisional republic to parliamentary federal government, then resigned when unauthorized provincial plebiscites for union with Germany placed domestic national aspiration against the obligations of Saint-Germain.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c9u5i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:40:00-06:00 -->Mayr died on 21 November 1922. His complete life, administrative scholarship, constitutional work, chancellorship, and resignation over the Tyrol plebiscite are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Later Greater German claims upon him as both Austrian federal founder and casualty of the suppressed demand for Anschluss remain extrapolative memory politics.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"scholar",title:"Historian and archivist",html:"<p>Born at Adlwang in Upper Austria on 10 April 1864, Mayr studied history and geography at the University of Vienna and completed a doctorate in 1890. Training at the Institute for Austrian Historical Research prepared him for archival work grounded in administrative records and legal documents.</p><p>He entered the Tyrolean archival service, directed the Tyrol State Archives from 1897 to 1920, and taught modern history at Innsbruck. His scholarly and administrative careers gave him an unusually concrete understanding of provincial rights.</p>"},
      {id:"imperial",title:"Imperial and Tyrolean politics",html:"<p>Mayr represented the Christian Social movement in the imperial Reichsrat from 1907 to 1911 and sat in the Tyrolean Landtag between 1908 and 1914. He approached politics through Catholic conservatism, provincial autonomy, and the legal continuity of administration.</p><p>Imperial collapse brought him into the Constituent National Assembly, where the design of the successor republic made his federal expertise central.</p>"},
      {id:"constitution",title:"The federal constitutional compromise",html:"<p>Mayr was assigned constitutional and administrative reform in October 1919. His Linz draft collected the demands of the Länder for a federal state with provincial institutions rather than a centralized continuation of German-Austria.</p><p><a href='karl-renner.html'>Karl Renner</a> and Mayr combined Social Democratic and Christian Social proposals in the Renner–Mayr draft. Their compromise supplied the working foundation for the <a href='federal-constitutional-law-1920.html'>Federal Constitutional Law</a> adopted on 1 October 1920.</p>"},
      {id:"chancellor",title:"State chancellor and federal chancellor",html:"<p>Mayr became state chancellor on 7 July 1920 at the head of a transitional proportional cabinet. After the Social Democrats withdrew and the <a href='1920-austrian-legislative-election.html'>October election</a> made Christian Socials the largest party, he formed a Christian Social minority government supported from the right.</p><p>When the constitution entered force on 10 November, the office became the federal chancellorship. Mayr also directed foreign affairs and oversaw the first operation of the National and Federal Councils.</p>"},
      {id:"government",title:"Governing the constitutional transition",html:"<p>The cabinet converted provisional ministries into federal institutions, negotiated urgent foreign credit, implemented the peace settlement, and confronted food, currency, and employment crises. <a href='walter-breisky.html'>Walter Breisky</a> served as vice-chancellor and directed education affairs.</p><p>Mayr’s government established the precedent of federal cabinets governing without Social Democratic participation. The constitutional settlement was consensual; the emerging cabinet system was not.</p>"},
      {id:"plebiscites",title:"Anschluss plebiscites and resignation",html:"<p>The <a href='treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html'>Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> prohibited union with Germany without international consent. Provincial activists nevertheless organized plebiscites in Tyrol and Salzburg during 1921 that returned overwhelming votes for Anschluss, while Styria prepared another.</p><p>Mayr regarded the votes as incompatible with the government’s foreign obligations and too dangerous to tolerate. Unable to make the provinces stop, he resigned; his cabinet left office on 21 June and was replaced by <a href='johannes-schober.html'>Johannes Schober</a>.</p>"},
      {id:"death",title:"Death and political memory",html:"<p>Mayr returned to parliamentary and scholarly life but died at Waldneukirchen on 21 May 1922, aged fifty-eight. He did not live to see the Geneva stabilization programme or the later destruction of the system he helped design.</p><p>Austrian constitutional history credits his federal draftsmanship. Pan-German memory emphasizes the plebiscites that ended his government; both readings omit part of a career spent trying to reconcile provincial German nationalism with an internationally viable Austrian state.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"federal-constitutional-law-1920.html",kicker:"Principal constitutional work",label:"Federal Constitutional Law"},{href:"1920-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Election as chancellor",label:"1920 Legislative Election"},{href:"karl-renner.html",kicker:"Constitutional collaborator",label:"Karl Renner"},{href:"walter-breisky.html",kicker:"Vice-chancellor",label:"Walter Breisky"},{href:"johannes-schober.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"Johannes Schober"},{href:"treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html",kicker:"Foreign constraint",label:"Treaty of Saint-Germain"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/person/1120",label:"Austrian Parliament — Michael Mayr biography and offices"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/historisches/100-jahre-b-vg/beratungsgrundlagen/",label:"Austrian Parliament — constitutional drafts and the Renner–Mayr compromise"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/unterlagen/Text_Renner-Mayr-Entwurf.pdf",label:"Austrian Parliament — transcript of the Renner–Mayr constitutional draft"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/bild/21000/2100082_800.jpg",label:"Austrian Parliament — Michael Mayr portrait"}],
    facts:[["Born","10 April 1864 · Adlwang"],["Died","21 May 1922 · Waldneukirchen"],["Profession","Historian, archivist, university professor"],["Party","Christian Social Party"],["Tyrol State Archives","Director · 1897–1920"],["Constituent Assembly","1919–1920"],["State chancellor","7 July–10 November 1920"],["Federal chancellor","10 November 1920–21 June 1921"],["Principal work","Federal Constitutional Law of 1920"],["Resignation issue","Unauthorized provincial Anschluss plebiscites"]]
  }),

  "walter-breisky":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"Walter Breisky",category:"People",eyebrow:"Civil servant, education reformer, and caretaker chancellor · born 1871",infoboxKicker:"Vice-chancellor and chancellor for one day",landscape:breiskyPortrait,
    lead:"Walter Breisky was an Austrian jurist and senior civil servant who served as vice-chancellor from 1920 to 1922, directed education policy, and headed a caretaker government for approximately twenty-four hours in January 1922. A Protestant administrator without a secure base in any political camp, he preserved parts of Otto Glöckel’s school reform while adding rural cultural access, teacher training, and administrative modernization. His brief chancellorship was a parliamentary maneuver surrounding ratification of the Treaty of Lana rather than an independent political programme.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-d1v6j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:45:00-06:00 -->Breisky’s life, civil-service and political career, retirement, withdrawal from public life, lack of support for the corporate dictatorship or Anschluss, and survival through 31 July 1941 are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His historical arrest and death in 1944 occur after the divergence and are not inherited automatically; his later residence, treatment by the regime, and death remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"bureaucrat",title:"A Habsburg civil servant",html:"<p>Born in Bern on 8 July 1871 to a family of Bohemian origin, Breisky was raised in Prague and Vienna. He studied law and political science at the University of Vienna and entered the Lower Austrian administration in 1895.</p><p>He advanced through the education ministry despite the disadvantage that his Protestant faith could present in a predominantly Catholic bureaucracy. By the First World War he was an experienced ministerial administrator.</p>"},
      {id:"republic",title:"Service to the new republic",html:"<p>Imperial collapse distressed the monarchically formed civil servant, but he remained at his post. Chancellor <a href='karl-renner.html'>Karl Renner</a> made him a senior director in the State Chancellery and relied on him for review of executive business.</p><p>Breisky’s transfer of loyalty illustrates how the <a href='republic-of-german-austria.html'>new republic</a> survived through personnel who had been trained by the monarchy. Administrative continuity did not imply ideological enthusiasm.</p>"},
      {id:"education",title:"Education policy",html:"<p><a href='michael-mayr.html'>Michael Mayr</a> appointed Breisky to direct education in July 1920. He worked with Social Democratic reformer Otto Glöckel, retaining innovations compatible with professional administration while moderating others that threatened established school structures.</p><p>Breisky promoted girls’ education, teacher training, school medical services, curriculum reform, transparent textbook approval, and access to concerts and theater in rural districts. His record resists a simple division between conservative obstruction and socialist innovation.</p>"},
      {id:"vice",title:"Vice-chancellor under Mayr and Schober",html:"<p>Breisky became vice-chancellor on 20 November 1920 and continued to supervise education under Mayr and <a href='johannes-schober.html'>Johannes Schober</a>. He temporarily directed interior, education, and military business when vacancies required an experienced administrator.</p><p>He was nominated through the Christian Social side of cabinet arrangements but never became a mass-party politician. His value lay in competence and acceptability across factions.</p>"},
      {id:"lana",title:"The Treaty of Lana crisis",html:"<p>Schober’s Treaty of Lana with Czechoslovakia promised credit and diplomatic normalization in exchange for renewed respect for the Saint-Germain order. Greater Germans opposed its explicit rejection of immediate Anschluss and withdrew their cabinet representative.</p><p>Schober resigned on 26 January 1922, releasing the Greater Germans from a coalition obligation to support government bills. President Michael Hainisch appointed Breisky caretaker chancellor, and the treaty passed with Christian Social and Social Democratic votes over German-national opposition.</p>"},
      {id:"day",title:"A chancellorship of twenty-four hours",html:"<p>Once the ratification maneuver had succeeded, Schober returned on 27 January. Breisky resumed the vice-chancellorship and education portfolio until the cabinet fell in May.</p><p>The episode exposes the procedural complexity of coalition government: the shortest Austrian chancellorship did exactly what its sponsors required. It neither had time nor authorization to develop a separate programme.</p>"},
      {id:"statistics",title:"Statistics and economic research",html:"<p>Breisky returned to the chancellery and became president of the Austrian Statistics Office in February 1923. He reorganized an understaffed service whose population, employment, and production data were essential to fiscal reconstruction.</p><p>He also supported creation of the Austrian Institute of Economic Research, establishing an external body capable of testing official economic assessments.</p>"},
      {id:"later",title:"Retirement and setting record",html:"<p>Failing eyesight led Breisky to retire in 1931. He lived at Klosterneuburg, participated in the Pan-Europe movement and charitable organizations, and withdrew still further after the destruction of parliamentary Austria and the Anschluss.</p><p>He is alive at the divergence. Greater German official memory reduces him to a one-day officeholder, while administrative history preserves a more substantial record in education, statistics, and the transfer of state capacity from empire to republic.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"michael-mayr.html",kicker:"Chancellor and cabinet chief",label:"Michael Mayr"},{href:"johannes-schober.html",kicker:"Predecessor and successor",label:"Johannes Schober"},{href:"treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html",kicker:"Treaty framework",label:"Treaty of Saint-Germain"},{href:"first-austrian-republic.html",kicker:"State served",label:"First Austrian Republic"},{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Parliamentary setting",label:"National Council"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/person/64227",label:"Austrian Parliament — Walter Breisky’s cabinet offices"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/personen/bundesregierung/bundesregierungen-seit-1918",label:"Austrian Parliament — federal governments and caretaker appointments since 1918"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/bild/47455/4745554_800.jpg",label:"Austrian Parliament — Walter Breisky portrait"}],
    facts:[["Born","8 July 1871 · Bern"],["Later fate","Unestablished from 1 August 1941"],["Profession","Jurist and civil servant"],["Religious affiliation","Protestant"],["Education portfolio","7 July 1920–31 May 1922"],["Vice-chancellor","20 November 1920–31 May 1922"],["Caretaker chancellor","26–27 January 1922"],["Immediate issue","Ratification of the Treaty of Lana"],["Statistics Office","President from 1923"],["Retired","1931"]]
  }),

  "rudolf-ramek":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"Rudolf Ramek",category:"People",eyebrow:"Christian Social chancellor and parliamentary president · 1881–1941",infoboxKicker:"Chancellor during introduction of the Schilling",landscape:ramekPortrait,
    lead:"Rudolf Ramek was an Austrian Christian Social lawyer and politician who served as federal chancellor from November 1924 to October 1926. His government introduced the Schilling, completed major fiscal and administrative reforms, expanded social insurance, and brought League of Nations financial supervision to an end. Banking failures and controversy around the state postal savings bank forced his resignation. As second president of the National Council in 1933, he later participated unintentionally in the procedural resignations used by Engelbert Dollfuss to disable Parliament.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e2w7k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:50:00-06:00 -->Ramek died on 24 July 1941, eight days before the divergence. His complete life, priesthood, parliamentary career, chancellorship, constitutional service, later corporate-state activity, and death are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His biography therefore requires no post-divergence personal chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"law",title:"Lawyer from Austrian Silesia",html:"<p>Born at Teschen in Austrian Silesia on 12 April 1881, Ramek studied law in Vienna and received his doctorate in 1907. He trained in Teschen, Innsbruck, and Meran before establishing a legal practice in Salzburg.</p><p>Work for church institutions and the Christian Social movement tied his professional career to Catholic politics. He became the party’s Salzburg provincial chairman in 1922.</p>"},
      {id:"founding",title:"Founding governments and Parliament",html:"<p>Ramek sat in the Constituent National Assembly and became state secretary for justice in Renner’s third government. He entered the National Council in 1920 and briefly directed the interior and education ministry in Mayr’s final cabinet.</p><p>These roles placed him inside the legal construction of the republic without making him one of its principal theorists. He was valued as a negotiator capable of holding Christian Social and coalition interests together.</p>"},
      {id:"cabinet",title:"Formation of the Ramek government",html:"<p>When <a href='ignaz-seipel.html'>Ignaz Seipel</a> resigned in November 1924, Ramek formed a coalition on the parliamentary basis created by the <a href='1923-austrian-legislative-election.html'>1923 election</a>. Christian Socials governed with German-national and agrarian support.</p><p>Ramek inherited reconstruction policies already in motion. His cabinet’s importance lies in converting emergency stabilization into ordinary currency, tax, administrative, and social law.</p>"},
      {id:"schilling",title:"The Schilling and financial sovereignty",html:"<p>The Schillingrechnungsgesetz took effect on 1 January 1925, fixing the new unit at 10,000 paper crowns. Banknotes entered circulation in March and coins followed. The hard currency became the most visible sign that hyperinflation had ended.</p><p>League of Nations financial supervision concluded in 1926. Austria recovered formal control over its finances, although dependence on foreign credit, unemployment, and the social cost of stabilization remained.</p>"},
      {id:"reform",title:"Constitutional, tax, and social reform",html:"<p>The government negotiated a 1925 constitutional and fiscal settlement adjusting the division of revenue and administrative competence between federation and Länder. Tax and administrative reforms accompanied the currency change.</p><p>New unemployment protection and old-age and invalidity insurance widened social provision. These measures complicate any description of the ministry as merely conservative retrenchment.</p>"},
      {id:"banks",title:"Banking failures and resignation",html:"<p>Currency stabilization exposed banks whose wartime inflation profits and speculative structures could not survive hard money. The Depositenbank, Biedermann Bank, Anglo-Austrian Bank, and other institutions failed or required intervention.</p><p>Controversy over the state-owned postal savings bank and political responsibility for financial rescue accumulated around the cabinet. Ramek resigned on 20 October 1926, and Seipel returned as chancellor.</p>"},
      {id:"president",title:"Second president of the National Council",html:"<p>After the <a href='1930-austrian-legislative-election.html'>1930 election</a>, Ramek became second president of the National Council. During the disputed vote of 4 March 1933, first president Karl Renner and then Ramek and third president Sepp Straffner resigned the chair so each could cast a ballot.</p><p>The resignations were temporary procedural acts. Dollfuss called them parliamentary self-elimination and used police to prevent the elected chamber from reconvening.</p>"},
      {id:"end",title:"The controlled session of 1934",html:"<p>Ramek presided when a government-controlled remainder of the National Council met on 30 April 1934 after Social Democratic deputies had been excluded. The session supplied a legal façade for the corporate constitution and abolition of the elected legislature.</p><p>He died in Vienna on 24 July 1941. His career links the republic’s constructive monetary settlement to the procedural vulnerability through which its Parliament was destroyed.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"1923-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Parliamentary basis",label:"1923 Legislative Election"},{href:"ignaz-seipel.html",kicker:"Predecessor and successor",label:"Ignaz Seipel"},{href:"geneva-protocols-1922.html",kicker:"Financial programme",label:"Geneva Protocols"},{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Later presidency",label:"National Council"},{href:"federal-state-of-austria.html",kicker:"1934 constitutional order",label:"Federal State of Austria"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/person/1526",label:"Austrian Parliament — Rudolf Ramek biography and offices"},{href:"https://www.oesta.gv.at/veroeffentlichungen/archivale-des-monats/die-einfuehrung-des-schillings-vor-100-jahren.html",label:"Austrian State Archives — introduction of the Schilling under Ramek"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/fachinfos/parlamentsarchiv/Die-harte-Waehrung-100-Jahre-Schillingrechnungsgesetz",label:"Austrian Parliament — one hundred years of the Schillingrechnungsgesetz"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/fachinfos/blick-ins-archiv/Transkript_Amtliches-Protokoll-der-125.-Sitzung-des-Nationalrates-vom-4.-Maerz-1933.pdf",label:"Austrian Parliament — official record of 4 March 1933"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/bild/21002/2100231_384.jpg",label:"Austrian Parliament — Rudolf Ramek portrait"}],
    facts:[["Born","12 April 1881 · Teschen"],["Died","24 July 1941 · Vienna"],["Profession","Lawyer"],["Party","Christian Social Party"],["Federal chancellor","20 November 1924–20 October 1926"],["Major monetary act","Introduction of the Schilling · 1925"],["League financial control","Ended 1926"],["National Council office","Second president · 1930–1933"],["March 1933 role","One of three presidents resigning the chair"]]
  }),

  "ernst-streeruwitz":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"Ernst Streeruwitz",category:"People",eyebrow:"Industrialist, parliamentarian, and chancellor · born 1874",infoboxKicker:"Chancellor during the 1929 constitutional crisis",landscape:streeruwitzPortrait,
    lead:"Ernst Streeruwitz was an Austrian former army officer, industrial manager, and Christian Social politician who served as chancellor and foreign minister from May to September 1929. Selected as a compromise after Ignaz Seipel’s resignation, he led a Christian Social–Greater German–Landbund coalition while negotiating constitutional reform with the Social Democrats. Heimwehr leaders and allies within his own governing camp judged that compromise insufficient and forced his replacement by Johannes Schober.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f3x8l writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:55:00-06:00 -->Streeruwitz’s life, military and industrial career, parliamentary activity, chancellorship, corporate-state service, pro-Anschluss position, and survival through 31 July 1941 are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His historical death in 1952 occurs after the divergence and is not inherited automatically; no later setting office, political activity, or death has been established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"officer",title:"Officer and industrial manager",html:"<p>Born at Mies in Bohemia on 23 September 1874, Streeruwitz attended the Theresian Military Academy and served as a cavalry officer before illness ended his active military career. He studied engineering and law and moved into industrial management.</p><p>Directing the Neunkirchen textile works and serving employers’ organizations made him a representative of the industrial wing of Christian Social politics rather than the clerical or provincial party apparatus.</p>"},
      {id:"parliament",title:"Industry in Parliament",html:"<p>Streeruwitz entered the National Council after the <a href='1923-austrian-legislative-election.html'>1923 election</a> and remained a deputy through 1934. He held leading positions in the industrial federation and Vienna Chamber of Commerce.</p><p>His speeches combined fiscal orthodoxy, employer interests, technocratic administration, and suspicion of class conflict. He possessed expertise but lacked an independent mass following.</p>"},
      {id:"formation",title:"A compromise chancellor",html:"<p>Seipel’s resignation in April 1929 opened a prolonged cabinet crisis. Streeruwitz took office on 4 May at the head of Christian Social, Greater German, and Landbund ministers, the same broad parliamentary alignment returned in the <a href='1927-austrian-legislative-election.html'>1927 election</a>.</p><p>The selection was intended to preserve coalition government without immediately conceding the Heimwehr demand for authoritarian reconstruction.</p>"},
      {id:"reform",title:"Negotiating constitutional reform",html:"<p>Heimwehr leaders demanded a powerful presidency, a corporate chamber, reduced parliamentary authority, and stronger security government. The SDAP held more than one third of the National Council and could block constitutional amendment.</p><p>Streeruwitz opened negotiations rather than attempt an illegal alteration. A compromise strengthening the executive while preserving Parliament began to emerge, but the Heimwehr called such accommodation tolerance of Marxism.</p>"},
      {id:"geneva",title:"Geneva diplomacy",html:"<p>As foreign minister, Streeruwitz attended the League of Nations Assembly in September 1929 and pursued relief from Austria’s remaining reparations obligations. Foreign credit made domestic threats of civil conflict especially damaging.</p><p>His absence gave coalition opponents room to coordinate. Heimwehr ultimatums and demonstrations around Vienna drove down confidence in the Schilling and made the cabinet appear unable to control its own supporters.</p>"},
      {id:"fall",title:"The Heimwehr ultimatum and resignation",html:"<p>On 20 September Heimwehr leaders openly threatened a coup if their constitutional programme was not adopted. Christian Social figures who wanted a stronger government withdrew support from Streeruwitz and converged on police president <a href='johannes-schober.html'>Johannes Schober</a> as a replacement.</p><p>Streeruwitz resigned on 25 September and remained in caretaker office until Schober was sworn in the next day. Schober eventually enacted the <a href='federal-constitutional-amendment-1929.html'>1929 amendment</a> through the parliamentary compromise the Heimwehr had rejected.</p>"},
      {id:"later",title:"Later politics and setting record",html:"<p>Streeruwitz remained in Parliament, business organizations, and conservative political circles. He supported the corporate state and later welcomed the Anschluss, positions consistent with his prewar hostility to party conflict even though his own cabinet had defended legal negotiation.</p><p>He completed a doctorate in political science in 1939 and is alive at the divergence. Reich accounts celebrate his national alignment while minimizing the coercion that ended his chancellorship.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"1927-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Parliamentary basis",label:"1927 Legislative Election"},{href:"ignaz-seipel.html",kicker:"Predecessor",label:"Ignaz Seipel"},{href:"johannes-schober.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"Johannes Schober"},{href:"heimwehr.html",kicker:"Government pressure",label:"Heimwehr"},{href:"federal-constitutional-amendment-1929.html",kicker:"Unfinished reform",label:"1929 Constitutional Amendment"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/person/1912",label:"Austrian Parliament — Ernst Streeruwitz biography and offices"},{href:"https://austria-forum.org/af/AEIOU/Streeruwitz%2C_Ernst",label:"Austria-Forum — Ernst Streeruwitz"},{href:"https://dspace.zcu.cz/bitstream/11025/34424/1/Hobelt.pdf",label:"Lothar Höbelt — Ernst von Streeruwitz, an Austrian chancellor"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/bild/21003/2100357_384.jpg",label:"Austrian Parliament — Ernst Streeruwitz portrait"}],
    facts:[["Born","23 September 1874 · Mies, Bohemia"],["Later fate","Unestablished from 1 August 1941"],["Professions","Army officer, industrial manager, politician"],["Party","Christian Social Party"],["National Council","1923–1934"],["Federal chancellor","4 May–26 September 1929"],["Foreign minister","Concurrent with chancellorship"],["Coalition","Christian Social–Greater German–Landbund"],["Government-ending issue","Heimwehr pressure over constitutional reform"]]
  }),

  "karl-buresch":firstRepublicGovernment({
    title:"Karl Buresch",category:"People",eyebrow:"Lawyer, provincial governor, and depression chancellor · 1878–1936",infoboxKicker:"Chancellor after the Creditanstalt collapse",landscape:bureschPortrait,
    lead:"Karl Buresch was an Austrian Christian Social lawyer and politician who served as governor of Lower Austria and as federal chancellor from June 1931 to May 1932. He inherited the Creditanstalt collapse, abandonment of the Austro-German customs-union project, and a state dependent on foreign emergency credit. His coalition survived the Pfrimer Putsch but lost its German-national partner and continued as a Christian Social minority cabinet before electoral setbacks brought Engelbert Dollfuss to office.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g4y9m writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 -->Buresch died on 16 September 1936. His complete life, provincial and parliamentary career, chancellorship, finance ministry, banking office, and death are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His service across parliamentary depression government and the authoritarian Federal State supports interpretation of institutional continuity across the 1933 rupture.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"local",title:"Lawyer and local politician",html:"<p>Born at Groß-Enzersdorf on 12 October 1878, Buresch studied law at the University of Vienna and received his doctorate in 1901. He established a legal practice in his home community, joined its council, and served as mayor from 1916 to 1919.</p><p>Municipal office during wartime shortages and imperial collapse prepared him for a political career centered on administration and coalition management rather than ideology.</p>"},
      {id:"province",title:"Lower Austrian governor",html:"<p>Buresch entered the Constituent National Assembly in 1919 and sat in the National Council from 1920 to 1934. He became governor of Lower Austria in 1922 and led the Christian Social parliamentary group from 1929.</p><p>Lower Austria surrounded <a href='red-vienna.html'>Red Vienna</a> geographically and opposed it politically. Buresch represented the provincial Catholic organization that sustained Christian Social federal government.</p>"},
      {id:"collapse",title:"Government after the Creditanstalt",html:"<p><a href='otto-ender.html'>Otto Ender</a> resigned when Parliament refused the emergency authority sought after the <a href='creditanstalt-collapse.html'>Creditanstalt collapse</a>. Seipel also failed to assemble a cabinet. President Wilhelm Miklas appointed Buresch chancellor on 20 June 1931.</p><p>The government joined Christian Social and German-national ministers. It had to guarantee banks, defend the Schilling, negotiate foreign support, and distribute losses that no domestic constituency wished to accept.</p>"},
      {id:"customs",title:"End of the customs-union project",html:"<p>Austria’s financial dependence gave France and other creditors leverage against the proposed customs union with Germany. The project was abandoned after international legal and diplomatic opposition, ending the central foreign initiative of the Ender–Schober cabinet.</p><p>Buresch sought emergency assistance without allowing formal insolvency. Every loan condition revived the question of whether Austrian sovereignty survived dependence on outside supervision.</p>"},
      {id:"pfrimer",title:"The Pfrimer Putsch",html:"<p>On 12 September 1931 Styrian Heimwehr commander Walter Pfrimer attempted to seize provincial power and march on Vienna. The <a href='pfrimer-putsch.html'>putsch</a> collapsed within a day when the army, police, other Heimwehr leaders, and the public failed to support it.</p><p>The government restored order without civil war, but the attempt exposed how openly authoritarian forces operated inside the conservative camp. Parliamentary partners drew different lessons from its failure.</p>"},
      {id:"minority",title:"Austerity and minority government",html:"<p>German-national ministers withdrew at the beginning of 1932, leaving Buresch to govern as a Christian Social minority. Spending cuts, taxes, wage pressure, unemployment, and restricted credit deepened the depression’s political cost.</p><p>The government negotiated the path toward a League of Nations loan, but final parliamentary approval came under Dollfuss. Foreign assistance was tied again to Austrian independence and the abandonment of union with Germany.</p>"},
      {id:"resignation",title:"Provincial elections and resignation",html:"<p>National Socialists made sharp gains in the Vienna, Lower Austrian, and Salzburg elections of April 1932. Christian Social losses weakened Buresch within his own party and demonstrated that parliamentary conservatism was losing voters to an explicitly German revolutionary movement.</p><p>He resigned on 20 May. <a href='engelbert-dollfuss.html'>Engelbert Dollfuss</a> formed a cabinet with a one-vote majority and inherited the same financial settlement.</p>"},
      {id:"finance",title:"Finance minister in the corporate state",html:"<p>Buresch returned as Lower Austrian governor and joined Dollfuss’s cabinet as finance minister in May 1933. He remained in office through parliamentary suppression, civil war, the July Putsch, and establishment of the <a href='federal-state-of-austria.html'>Federal State</a>.</p><p>After leaving the ministry in 1935, he headed the postal savings bank. He died in Vienna on 16 September 1936. His career shows that the personnel managing democratic austerity also supplied administrative continuity to dictatorship.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"creditanstalt-collapse.html",kicker:"Inherited financial crisis",label:"Creditanstalt Collapse"},{href:"otto-ender.html",kicker:"Predecessor",label:"Otto Ender"},{href:"pfrimer-putsch.html",kicker:"Domestic crisis",label:"Pfrimer Putsch"},{href:"engelbert-dollfuss.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"Engelbert Dollfuss"},{href:"federal-state-of-austria.html",kicker:"Later government",label:"Federal State of Austria"},{href:"1930-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Parliamentary basis",label:"1930 Legislative Election"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/person/147",label:"Austrian Parliament — Karl Buresch biography and offices"},{href:"https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/sfz7493.html",label:"Neue Deutsche Biographie — Karl Buresch and the depression government"},{href:"https://hdgoe.at/lausanner_voelkerbundanleihe_1932",label:"House of Austrian History — the 1932 League of Nations loan"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/bild/20995/2099563_800.jpg",label:"Austrian Parliament — Karl Buresch portrait"}],
    facts:[["Born","12 October 1878 · Groß-Enzersdorf"],["Died","16 September 1936 · Vienna"],["Profession","Lawyer"],["Party","Christian Social Party"],["Governor of Lower Austria","1922–1931; 1932–1933"],["Federal chancellor","20 June 1931–20 May 1932"],["Principal crisis","Creditanstalt collapse"],["Domestic challenge","Pfrimer Putsch · September 1931"],["Finance minister","1933–1935"],["Successor as chancellor","Engelbert Dollfuss"]]
  })
});

